WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.280
Your DNA stays inside the nucleus, wound on

00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:05.120
histones, as you saw last time.

00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:08.880
But the machines that build proteins, the ribosomes,

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:11.760
sit outside it, in the cytoplasm.

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:13.680
So how do the instructions in a gene

00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:15.600
get from one place to the other, when

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:18.240
the DNA itself never leaves?

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:20.480
The cell sends out a copy.

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:23.360
Today, how a cell copies one gene, and

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:25.120
why it bothers to copy at all.

00:00:26.334 --> 00:00:28.893
A gene is a section of DNA whose

00:00:28.974 --> 00:00:31.933
order of bases codes for a product, usually

00:00:32.014 --> 00:00:34.973
a polypeptide, a chain of amino acids that

00:00:35.054 --> 00:00:36.693
folds into a protein.

00:00:37.054 --> 00:00:39.373
To use a gene, the cell copies its

00:00:39.614 --> 00:00:42.333
bases into a single strand of RNA called

00:00:42.494 --> 00:00:45.453
messenger RNA, or mRNA.

00:00:45.534 --> 00:00:48.053
That copying is called transcription.

00:00:48.494 --> 00:00:50.413
Think of a recipe book that is never

00:00:50.614 --> 00:00:52.173
allowed out of the library.

00:00:52.254 --> 00:00:54.173
You copy the one page you need, and

00:00:54.254 --> 00:00:56.173
carry the copy to the kitchen.

00:00:56.214 --> 00:00:58.013
The book stays safe, and you can make

00:00:58.094 --> 00:00:59.693
as many copies as you like.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:04.180
RNA is a nucleic acid like DNA, built

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:07.260
from nucleotides, but with three differences.

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:09.700
It is a single strand, not a double

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:10.540
helix.

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:14.420
Its sugar is ribose, not deoxyribose, which is

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:17.220
what the R in RNA stands for.

00:01:17.260 --> 00:01:20.180
And it uses a base called uracil, U,

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:22.740
in place of thymine, T.

00:01:22.820 --> 00:01:25.460
U pairs with A, just as T does,

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:28.260
so the pairing rule becomes A with U,

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:29.700
and C with G.

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:33.220
The copying is done by an enzyme called

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:34.820
RNA polymerase.

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:37.220
First it has to find where the gene

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:38.020
starts.

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:40.660
Just before each gene is a stretch of

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:43.780
DNA called the promoter, a landing site that

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:45.620
the polymerase binds to.

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:48.340
Once it has bound, the polymerase pulls the

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:51.540
two DNA strands apart over a short stretch,

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:54.340
breaking the hydrogen bonds between the bases, so

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:56.020
the bases are exposed.

00:01:57.234 --> 00:01:58.713
Here is the key point.

00:01:58.994 --> 00:02:01.473
The polymerase copies only one of the two

00:02:01.554 --> 00:02:04.353
strands, called the template strand.

00:02:04.394 --> 00:02:06.913
The other one, the coding strand, is not

00:02:06.994 --> 00:02:07.953
read.

00:02:08.034 --> 00:02:10.913
One strand is enough, because the template fixes

00:02:10.994 --> 00:02:13.633
every base of the mRNA, just as one

00:02:13.794 --> 00:02:16.753
DNA strand fixed its partner when DNA was

00:02:16.834 --> 00:02:17.313
copied.

00:02:18.534 --> 00:02:19.653
Now the direction.

00:02:20.134 --> 00:02:23.093
The polymerase can only add each new nucleotide

00:02:23.174 --> 00:02:26.373
to the 3' end of the growing mRNA,

00:02:26.534 --> 00:02:29.413
so the mRNA grows from its 5' end

00:02:29.574 --> 00:02:30.853
to its 3' end.

00:02:31.414 --> 00:02:34.613
And because paired strands run in opposite directions,

00:02:34.654 --> 00:02:36.933
the polymerase reads the template from its 3'

00:02:37.014 --> 00:02:39.093
end to its 5' end.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:41.260
Let's copy one.

00:02:41.740 --> 00:02:43.900
Here is a template strand, with its 3'

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:47.020
end on the left: T A C, C

00:02:47.100 --> 00:02:48.860
G A, A T T.

00:02:49.420 --> 00:02:51.900
The polymerase starts at the 3' end and

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:52.780
moves right.

00:02:53.340 --> 00:02:56.580
Free RNA nucleotides drift in, and only a

00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:57.900
matching one stays.

00:02:58.540 --> 00:03:00.060
T needs A.

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:03.100
A needs U, not T, because this is

00:03:03.260 --> 00:03:03.900
RNA.

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:05.740
C needs G.

00:03:06.380 --> 00:03:09.100
The next three: C with G, G with

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:10.700
C, A with U.

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:14.060
The last three: A with U, T with

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:15.580
A, T with A.

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:19.020
Each new nucleotide joins the 3' end of

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:20.380
the one before.

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:23.660
The mRNA reads A U G, G C

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:26.940
U, U A A, from its 5' end.

00:03:28.167 --> 00:03:30.086
Now look at the strand the polymerase did

00:03:30.247 --> 00:03:32.326
not read, the coding strand.

00:03:32.807 --> 00:03:36.086
It reads A T G, G C T,

00:03:36.247 --> 00:03:38.646
T A A, from its 5' end.

00:03:39.207 --> 00:03:42.406
Compare it with the mRNA: the same letters,

00:03:42.447 --> 00:03:45.046
in the same direction, except that T has

00:03:45.127 --> 00:03:45.926
become U.

00:03:46.567 --> 00:03:48.606
So the mRNA is a copy of the

00:03:48.647 --> 00:03:51.446
coding strand, built by pairing with the template

00:03:51.527 --> 00:03:52.006
strand.

00:03:52.647 --> 00:03:55.206
That is the trap in many exam questions,

00:03:55.287 --> 00:03:57.606
so always check which strand you have been

00:03:57.767 --> 00:03:58.166
given.

00:03:59.367 --> 00:04:01.446
At the end of the gene, the polymerase

00:04:01.527 --> 00:04:05.046
lets go, and the new mRNA comes away.

00:04:05.207 --> 00:04:08.086
Behind the polymerase, the two DNA strands have

00:04:08.167 --> 00:04:10.566
already paired up again, so the gene is

00:04:10.767 --> 00:04:13.526
unchanged and ready to be copied again.

00:04:13.607 --> 00:04:16.166
In a cell like ours, the mRNA leaves

00:04:16.247 --> 00:04:18.886
the nucleus through a nuclear pore, a small

00:04:18.967 --> 00:04:21.686
opening in the nuclear envelope, and travels out

00:04:21.727 --> 00:04:22.886
to the ribosomes.

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:26.820
One gene can be copied many times, so

00:04:26.860 --> 00:04:28.420
the cell can make a lot of one

00:04:28.500 --> 00:04:30.100
protein quickly.

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:32.740
And each mRNA is broken down after a

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:35.060
while, so the cell can stop making a

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:37.060
protein by stopping the copying.

00:04:38.267 --> 00:04:40.986
Transcription can look like DNA copying.

00:04:41.467 --> 00:04:43.946
Both open up the DNA, and both build

00:04:43.987 --> 00:04:45.986
a new strand by base pairing on a

00:04:46.027 --> 00:04:47.146
template.

00:04:47.227 --> 00:04:50.106
But replication copies both strands of all the

00:04:50.187 --> 00:04:54.346
DNA, into new DNA, before a cell divides.

00:04:54.427 --> 00:04:57.866
Transcription copies one strand of one gene, into

00:04:57.947 --> 00:05:00.906
RNA with U, whenever the cell needs that

00:05:01.027 --> 00:05:02.426
gene's product.

00:05:02.507 --> 00:05:05.706
In one sentence: both use a DNA template,

00:05:05.827 --> 00:05:09.466
whereas only transcription makes RNA, from one strand

00:05:09.547 --> 00:05:10.586
of one gene.

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:14.680
The mRNA is read in groups of three

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:16.600
bases, called codons.

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:20.680
Our mRNA has three: A U G, G

00:05:20.760 --> 00:05:23.160
C U and U A A.

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:26.080
What each codon means is the next video.

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:28.440
But one consequence follows already.

00:05:28.760 --> 00:05:31.480
In a gene such as CFTR, the gene

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:34.400
linked to cystic fibrosis, if one base in

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:37.400
the template strand changes, the matching base in

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:40.840
the mRNA changes too, so a codon changes

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:42.840
before any protein is made.

00:05:44.067 --> 00:05:45.506
Two things to remember.

00:05:45.747 --> 00:05:49.106
First, transcription keeps the language.

00:05:49.427 --> 00:05:52.946
DNA and RNA are both written in bases,

00:05:53.027 --> 00:05:54.626
so it is like copying notes in the

00:05:54.707 --> 00:05:55.786
same language.

00:05:56.067 --> 00:06:00.226
Translation, next, changes the language, from bases to

00:06:00.307 --> 00:06:01.586
amino acids.

00:06:01.907 --> 00:06:06.466
Second, the trap: mRNA matches the coding strand,

00:06:06.507 --> 00:06:08.946
but it is built on the template strand.

00:06:10.167 --> 00:06:11.766
Two harder questions now.

00:06:12.327 --> 00:06:14.526
First, here is a short section of a

00:06:14.567 --> 00:06:17.686
gene, both strands, with their ends labelled.

00:06:18.487 --> 00:06:21.046
RNA polymerase binds at the promoter on the

00:06:21.127 --> 00:06:22.886
left and moves to the right.

00:06:23.527 --> 00:06:25.846
Which strand is the template, and how does

00:06:25.927 --> 00:06:27.046
the direction tell you?

00:06:28.007 --> 00:06:31.446
Then write the mRNA, with its 5' and

00:06:31.607 --> 00:06:32.646
3' ends.

00:06:33.127 --> 00:06:34.406
Pause here to answer.

00:06:44.834 --> 00:06:47.153
The polymerase reads its template from the 3'

00:06:47.314 --> 00:06:49.313
end to the 5' end.

00:06:49.794 --> 00:06:52.033
It moves right, so the template has its

00:06:52.114 --> 00:06:54.753
3' end on the left, the top strand.

00:06:55.394 --> 00:06:59.313
Pairing it gives the mRNA A C G,

00:06:59.554 --> 00:07:02.033
U U A, G C C, from its

00:07:02.194 --> 00:07:03.073
5' end.

00:07:03.714 --> 00:07:05.713
If you paired the bottom strand, you paired

00:07:05.754 --> 00:07:08.033
the coding strand, and the message comes out

00:07:08.114 --> 00:07:08.673
wrong.

00:07:09.900 --> 00:07:10.860
Second.

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:14.220
A toxin from a mushroom stops RNA polymerase

00:07:14.300 --> 00:07:16.300
moving along DNA.

00:07:16.340 --> 00:07:18.940
In an affected liver cell, the DNA is

00:07:19.060 --> 00:07:22.140
not damaged and the ribosomes still work.

00:07:22.260 --> 00:07:24.460
Explain why the cell makes less and less

00:07:24.540 --> 00:07:27.340
of its proteins over the following hours.

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:28.780
Pause here to answer.

00:07:39.200 --> 00:07:42.640
With the polymerase stuck, no new mRNA is

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:43.360
made.

00:07:43.600 --> 00:07:46.560
The ribosomes can't read the DNA, which stays

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:49.920
in the nucleus, so they depend on mRNA.

00:07:50.560 --> 00:07:53.440
The mRNA already made is broken down over

00:07:53.640 --> 00:07:57.040
time, and nothing replaces it, so protein production

00:07:57.200 --> 00:07:57.760
falls.

00:07:58.480 --> 00:08:00.480
If you wrote that the cell can't copy

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:03.040
its DNA, that's replication.

00:08:03.520 --> 00:08:05.840
The toxin stops transcription.

00:08:07.067 --> 00:08:09.626
So when you're given a DNA strand, ask

00:08:09.707 --> 00:08:12.426
first: is it the template or the coding

00:08:12.507 --> 00:08:13.226
strand?

00:08:13.467 --> 00:08:15.626
Pair the template, with A to U.

00:08:16.107 --> 00:08:19.066
Copy the coding strand, swapping T for U.

00:08:19.547 --> 00:08:23.866
Then check the ends: mRNA runs 5' to

00:08:23.987 --> 00:08:24.586
3'.

00:08:25.067 --> 00:08:28.026
Next, the mRNA reaches a ribosome.

00:08:28.507 --> 00:08:30.346
How does a ribosome turn a code of

00:08:30.427 --> 00:08:32.986
bases into a chain of amino acids?

00:08:34.201 --> 00:08:35.640
Here are the key notes.

00:08:35.881 --> 00:08:37.800
Pause the video if you need more time

00:08:37.881 --> 00:08:38.680
to copy them.
